# estate-map

Never answer "what is running?" from memory. Run this.

## The apps

```bash
{{ platform.cli }} apps list
```

What this estate declares, from `estate.yaml`:

| key | app | probe | healthy | notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{{#services}}| {{key}} | `{{app}}` | `{{url}}` | {{expect}} |{{note_suffix}} |
{{/services}}
An app in that table missing from the list, or showing suspended, is an issue.
Anything in the list that is not in the table is not this agent's business.

## Is it serving

```bash
{{#services}}# {{key}}
{{ platform.cli }} status -a {{app}}
# NO -L. Never follow the redirect.
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' {{url}}
{{/services}}```

**Healthy is not always "200".** A service can answer for itself with a `307` to
a login page, or a `404` on a path that was never there. Both prove the app is
up and routing. Follow a login redirect with `-L` and you get `200` from the
login page, which is green whether the app works or not, so do not follow it.

So: the server answering for itself is healthy - `2xx`, `3xx`, `401`, `403`,
`404`. A `5xx` or no answer at all is not. `bin/pulse.sh` implements exactly
this and runs every 15 minutes with no model attached.

Anything narrower than that rule is in the notes column above, per service.
That column is the only place estate-specific health knowledge belongs; when
you learn something new about a service, put it in `estate.yaml` and run
`bin/render`, not in this file.

## The repos

```bash
gh repo list {{ github.owner }} --limit 30 --json name,pushedAt,isArchived \
  --jq '.[] | select(.isArchived==false) | "\(.pushedAt[0:10])  \(.name)"' | sort -r
```

`{{ github.repo }}` is the platform. `$HERMES_HOME` is this estate's home.

## The board

```bash
gh issue list -R {{ github.slug }} --state open \
  --json number,title,labels --jq '.[] | "#\(.number) \(.title) [\([.labels[].name]|join(","))]"'
```

## Thresholds

These live here, in the skill, not in a script. Changing what counts as
unhealthy is a one-line edit to this file with a diff, reviewable like anything
else. A threshold buried in code is a decision nobody can see.

| what | healthy | open an issue when |
|---|---|---|
{{#services}}| {{key}} probe | {{expect}} | two consecutive 5xx or no-answer, 15 min apart |
{{/services}}| machines started | all | any machine not `started` for 10 minutes |
| agent-go age | under 3 days | older than 7 days, untouched |
| open issues | any | a jump of more than 20 in a day |

One failed probe is a reading. Two are a fact. The threshold is two, everywhere,
and that is the whole reason the pulse runs every 15 minutes rather than hourly.

## Report shape

Two lines, then stop.

```
Platform: <serving or not>, <the number that says so>
Board: <n> open, <n> in agent-go, <n> pr-open
```
